!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/class2/schema/invalid/a-marked-name-cannot-be-applied-as-a-body-expected.tn"
!!schema:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/schema-sidecar.tn"
{
  spec: "[TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2, [TSON-SCHEMA] §12.1"
  description: """
    The companion of the bare case, reached through a parameter list. `abstract<text>` applies a template
    of that name as a whole declaration body, and the mark takes the head before the arguments are seen.

    The two cannot be told apart where the mark is read -- `abstract <T> { … }` opens with the same two
    tokens -- so what separates them is whether a type definition follows the parameters. A processor
    deciding on the head alone reports something about the closing brace instead, which is the observable
    difference and the reason this vector is separate from the bare one.

    Declaring the template is fine, and so is referencing it at any type position; naming it as an entire
    declaration body is the single thing the marks reserve.
    """
  meta: "meta.tn"
  import: ["core.tn"]
  error: {
    category: resolver
  }
}
